{"status":"answered","answer":"The owner cannot ignore the frozen holdout in the production checklist because the current release policy requires evaluating the production model changes on a frozen holdout. The legacy memo, which was superseded by the release policy, promoted models based solely on accuracy exceeding 0.80 without requiring a frozen holdout evaluation. However, the current policy mandates using F1 and ROC-AUC on a frozen holdout before promoting a new model.

The legacy memo is superseded by the release-policy, which specifies the current requirements for model promotion.
","sources":[{"source_id":"release-policy","quote":"Production model changes require an evaluation on a frozen holdout. Promote only when F1 improves by at least 0.005 and ROC-AUC drops by no more than 0.01. A drift alert alone never authorizes promotion. Keep the previous production version for rollback. The release approver is the project owner."},{"source_id":"legacy-policy","quote":"This document is historical. The old 2024 process promoted any model when accuracy exceeded 0.80. It is superseded by release-policy: current releases use F1 and ROC-AUC on a frozen holdout. Do not apply the old rule."}]}
